DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aattaann22(), aattaann22ff(), and aattaann22ll() functions compute the principal value
     of the arc tangent of _y_/_x, using the signs of both arguments to determine
     the quadrant of the return value.

     The ccaarrgg(), ccaarrggff(), and ccaarrggll() functions compute the complex argument
     (or phase angle) of _z.  The complex argument is the number theta such
     that z = r * e^(I * theta), where r = cabs(z).  The call carg(z) is
     equivalent to atan2(cimag(z), creal(z)), and similarly for ccaarrggff() and
     ccaarrggll().

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The aattaann22(), aattaann22ff(), and aattaann22ll() functions, if successful, return the
     arc tangent of _y_/_x in the range [-π, +π] radians.  Here are some of the
     special cases:

     aattaann22(_y, _x) :=       aattaann(_y_/_x)                       if _x > 0,
                          sign(_y)*(π - aattaann(|_y_/_x|))       if _x < 0,
                          0                               if x = y = 0, or
                          sign(_y)*π/2                     if _x = 0 ≠ _y.

NNOOTTEESS
     The function aattaann22() defines "if x > 0," aattaann22(_0, _0) = 0 despite that
     previously aattaann22(_0, _0) may have generated an error message.  The reasons
     for assigning a value to aattaann22(_0, _0) are these:

           1.   Programs that test arguments to avoid computing aattaann22(_0, _0)
                must be indifferent to its value.  Programs that require it to
                be invalid are vulnerable to diverse reactions to that inva‐
                lidity on diverse computer systems.

           2.   The aattaann22() function is used mostly to convert from rectangu‐
                lar (x,y) to polar (r,theta) coordinates that must satisfy x =
                r∗cos theta and y = r∗sin theta.  These equations are satis‐
                fied when (x=0,y=0) is mapped to (r=0,theta=0).  In general,
                conversions to polar coordinates should be computed thus:

                      r    := hypot(x,y);  ... := sqrt(x∗x+y∗y)
                      theta     := atan2(y,x).

           3.   The foregoing formulas need not be altered to cope in a rea‐
                sonable way with signed zeros and infinities on a machine that
                conforms to IEEE 754; the versions of hypot(3) and aattaann22()
                provided for such a machine are designed to handle all cases.
                That is why aattaann22(_±_0, _-_0) = ±π for instance.  In general the
                formulas above are equivalent to these:

                      r := sqrt(x∗x+y∗y); if r = 0 then x := copysign(1,x);

